1
00:00:00,540 --> 00:00:05,453
Chancellor, if you could switch jobs, okay.

2
00:00:05,503 --> 00:00:10,822
with Jensen for even a day, what would you do first?

3
00:00:10,872 --> 00:00:12,735
Oh, that's a great idea.

4
00:00:12,785 --> 00:00:14,298
Change jobs.

5
00:00:14,348 --> 00:00:15,738
Would you like to go there?

6
00:00:15,788 --> 00:00:19,418
No. Well, thank you.

7
00:00:19,468 --> 00:00:21,611
I...I'll give you my answer.

8
00:00:21,661 --> 00:00:25,589
I'd better put on my leather jacket.

9
00:00:25,639 --> 00:00:29,180
I would take a plane to Berlin.

10
00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:32,649
I would like to meet the German Chancellor.

11
00:00:32,699 --> 00:00:36,317
And I would tell him I was ready to invest.

12
00:00:36,367 --> 00:00:40,015
Well, that's exactly what I do.

13
00:00:40,065 --> 00:00:43,106
I think you could arrange a meeting, huh?

14
00:00:43,156 --> 00:00:45,959
Nobody...nobody invested more in Germany.

15
00:00:46,009 --> 00:00:46,993
Thank you for that.

16
00:00:47,043 --> 00:00:48,731
We are truly very grateful.

17
00:00:48,781 --> 00:00:52,295
Thank you for doing this, it's a great signal for our country.

18
00:00:52,345 --> 00:00:53,059
Thank you.

19
00:00:53,109 --> 00:00:54,104
Thank you.

20
00:00:54,154 --> 00:00:55,528
You know what?

21
00:00:55,578 --> 00:01:00,900
The history of Nvidia and the history of Germany played a very big role.

22
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:06,572
In fact, in 2000, Nvidia was a consumer-focused technology company.

23
00:01:06,622 --> 00:01:09,013
We only created products for PC gaming.

24
00:01:09,063 --> 00:01:18,068
And it was a company in Germany, Elsa, whose partnership led us to workstations, which in turn led us to all the car companies.

25
00:01:18,118 --> 00:01:26,465
And today we collaborate with automakers: from style, design, engineering, modeling, production, and even the cars themselves.

26
00:01:26,515 --> 00:01:32,338
It was in 2005 that Audi brought us into the automotive industry, right into the car.

27
00:01:32,388 --> 00:01:35,331
And it was 2023.

28
00:01:35,381 --> 00:01:37,586
Was it 2023?

29
00:01:37,636 --> 00:01:38,627
2020?

30
00:01:38,677 --> 00:01:44,571
Yes, in 2020, Ola and I entered into a partnership to create autonomous vehicles.

31
00:01:44,621 --> 00:01:48,473
So, Germany and Nvidia have been very close for a long time.

32
00:01:48,523 --> 00:01:55,915
Jensen, I think I already heard that you wouldn't want to change jobs, so I won't even ask what you would do as Chancellor.

33
00:01:55,965 --> 00:02:04,455
We'll leave you to yourself for now, and I suspect you're already seeing things that we may not even imagine yet.

34
00:02:04,505 --> 00:02:08,440
So, take us to the engineering office or the factory of the future.

35
00:02:08,490 --> 00:02:09,773
What do you see?

36
00:02:09,823 --> 00:02:11,915
What will it look like?

37
00:02:11,965 --> 00:02:14,659
Oh, that's interesting.

38
00:02:14,709 --> 00:02:19,389
In fact, every company in the future will have two factories.

39
00:02:19,439 --> 00:02:23,363
A factory that is real and a factory that is digital.

40
00:02:23,413 --> 00:02:28,865
And the digital factory will be so real that it can be considered real.

41
00:02:28,915 --> 00:02:38,089
If you create a digital twin of your company, your car company, that digital twin could be very real.

42
00:02:38,139 --> 00:02:48,198
And so, in the future, I believe that most people here in Germany will mostly work in a digital twin of their company.

43
00:02:48,248 --> 00:02:50,503
They will build digital cars.

44
00:02:50,553 --> 00:02:54,015
These cars will have digital brains.

45
00:02:54,065 --> 00:02:56,804
Production, factories will have digital, of course,

46
00:02:56,854 --> 00:03:04,609
robots with artificial intelligence that will work in robotic enterprises and build cars that will also be robotic.

47
00:03:04,659 --> 00:03:07,693
And everything will be completely controlled by software.

48
00:03:07,743 --> 00:03:10,498
Everything will be completely controlled by artificial intelligence.

49
00:03:10,548 --> 00:03:12,873
So, each company will create two things.

50
00:03:12,923 --> 00:03:18,214
They will create " brains" —intelligent systems that will be integrated into the products they supply.

51
00:03:18,264 --> 00:03:19,914
And therefore, Fascinating.

52
00:03:19,964 --> 00:03:21,284
Yes, that would be cool.

53
00:03:21,334 --> 00:03:29,203
Mr. Chancellor, of course, your vision is to introduce artificial intelligence into everyday use throughout German industry:

54
00:03:29,253 --> 00:03:32,284
from large manufacturers, the " Mittelstand," to smaller firms.

55
00:03:32,334 --> 00:03:42,194
Could you share some concrete examples of how you and the German government are ensuring that this broad implementation actually happens?

56
00:03:42,244 --> 00:03:43,166
Of course.

57
00:03:43,216 --> 00:03:53,528
First of all, I would like to say that I am very impressed with what Nvidia and German companies are already doing in this country.

58
00:03:53,578 --> 00:03:57,708
Ola Kallenius just handed me a model of the new Mercedes S-Class,

59
00:03:57,758 --> 00:04:06,305
promising that the car will have autonomous driving functionality in Germany by the end of this year.

60
00:04:06,355 --> 00:04:09,204
The safest and most autonomous.

61
00:04:09,254 --> 00:04:12,801
So this is happening in Germany.

62
00:04:12,851 --> 00:04:21,513
We in the government are fully committed to becoming one of

63
00:04:21,563 --> 00:04:25,415
the most advanced places in the world—not just in the European Union,

64
00:04:25,465 --> 00:04:32,613
but globally—for working with artificial intelligence and integrating AI into our industry.

65
00:04:32,663 --> 00:04:40,052
And one of the biggest, if not the biggest, advantage that we have in Germany is our so-called " Mittelstand."

66
00:04:40,102 --> 00:04:49,564
And I'm very happy to see that many small and medium-sized companies from Germany are here tonight.

67
00:04:49,614 --> 00:04:56,152
Please open your doors for artificial intelligence to use your data.

68
00:04:56,202 --> 00:05:01,392
This is extremely important for the future of Germany.

69
00:05:01,442 --> 00:05:12,262
This is the real secret and a real advantage for Germany— to use all the data that is already available in our companies.

70
00:05:12,312 --> 00:05:17,880
And if we could combine this artificial intelligence with companies

71
00:05:17,930 --> 00:05:24,567
like yours and our companies in Germany—both large and Mittelstand.

72
00:05:24,617 --> 00:05:29,898
I am very optimistic that we will be one of the leading,

73
00:05:29,948 --> 00:05:35,752
if not the leading country in the world in terms of the pace of deployment of artificial intelligence for all our companies:

74
00:05:35,802 --> 00:05:41,908
manufacturers, the engineering and automotive industries, as well as their suppliers.

75
00:05:41,958 --> 00:05:46,562
I think this is a great opportunity, and I am enthusiastic about its implementation.

76
00:05:46,612 --> 00:05:49,376
Yes, you are absolutely right.

77
00:05:49,426 --> 00:05:54,049
So, first of all,

78
00:05:54,099 --> 00:06:04,312
Germany's data is part of your natural resources.

79
00:06:04,362 --> 00:06:08,891
They belong to the nation.

80
00:06:08,941 --> 00:06:15,401
Data, your digital experience or what is otherwise called “ data”—your country’s data—is your culture,

81
00:06:15,451 --> 00:06:21,546
your values, your knowledge, your experience; it is your natural resource.

82
00:06:21,596 --> 00:06:25,041
He belongs to the nation.

83
00:06:25,091 --> 00:06:36,326
And you are absolutely right that you should import the technology to collect this data and turn it into your national intelligence.

84
00:06:36,376 --> 00:06:44,260
But you should not export your culture, your values, your knowledge, your history.

85
00:06:44,310 --> 00:06:49,295
And then importing German intelligence back—that makes no sense.

86
00:06:49,345 --> 00:06:51,881
So, that's the first thing.

87
00:06:51,931 --> 00:06:59,953
And so you should, you have every right, of course, to use the best AI technologies and services available in the world.

88
00:07:00,003 --> 00:07:02,965
But you have every opportunity to create your own AI.

89
00:07:03,015 --> 00:07:04,347
So, that's the first thing.

90
00:07:04,397 --> 00:07:11,436
The second is the next wave of AI—it's physical AI.

91
00:07:11,486 --> 00:07:15,595
This is the area where AI interacts with the physical world.

92
00:07:15,645 --> 00:07:20,280
Robotics, manufacturing, automobiles, heavy industry.

93
00:07:20,330 --> 00:07:25,705
This will never be replaced by digital technology.

94
00:07:25,755 --> 00:07:34,295
The work you know belongs to you, that knowledge belongs to you.

95
00:07:34,345 --> 00:07:39,638
Expertise in your field belongs to you.

96
00:07:39,688 --> 00:07:44,549
General artificial intelligence will never replace industry expertise.

97
00:07:44,599 --> 00:07:50,568
You can be as intelligent a general intelligence as you want.

98
00:07:50,618 --> 00:07:59,453
You will never be as smart as someone who is very, very specialized in a specific area of electromechanical technology.

99
00:07:59,503 --> 00:08:01,956
This cannot be replaced.

100
00:08:02,006 --> 00:08:04,971
Therefore, AI can only expand your capabilities.

101
00:08:05,021 --> 00:08:06,720
It will not replace your potential.

102
00:08:06,770 --> 00:08:09,532
So take advantage of this technology.

103
00:08:09,582 --> 00:08:16,821
This is a new tool.

104
00:08:16,871 --> 00:08:23,499
On some of these advantages, Jensen, we have, on the one hand, decades of industrial data.

105
00:08:23,549 --> 00:08:27,773
We have a very, let's say, qualified workforce.

106
00:08:27,823 --> 00:08:30,596
We have engineering experience.

107
00:08:30,646 --> 00:08:36,367
So this certainly, as you seem to have noted, shows great benefits for Germany.

108
00:08:36,417 --> 00:08:38,111
You have a global perspective.

109
00:08:38,161 --> 00:08:43,222
However, there are other countries that also have strengths and would also like to take advantage of them.

110
00:08:43,272 --> 00:08:48,284
You communicate with many governments around the world, with many companies around the world.

111
00:08:48,334 --> 00:08:52,866
Taking this global perspective,

112
00:08:52,916 --> 00:09:00,984
how ready is Germany for this next race in AI?

113
00:09:01,034 --> 00:09:12,537
As you know, the last great industrial revolution was electrification before the one I will describe.

114
00:09:12,587 --> 00:09:17,203
Electrification has led to the emergence of three true geographical leaders.

115
00:09:17,253 --> 00:09:24,570
Germany, the United States, Japan.

116
00:09:24,620 --> 00:09:35,279
Over the past 50 years, due to the current industrial revolution, which is computing and digital technology,

117
00:09:35,329 --> 00:09:44,474
the US has largely exported its heavy manufacturing, construction, craftsmanship, essentially to China.

118
00:09:44,524 --> 00:09:47,635
So, now there are three leaders in the world again.

119
00:09:47,685 --> 00:09:55,469
But this is Germany, Japan and China.

120
00:09:55,519 --> 00:09:59,524
China's advantage is that their industrial revolution, if you will,

121
00:09:59,574 --> 00:10:05,890
their economic transformation, happened precisely in the era of the computer industry.

122
00:10:05,940 --> 00:10:10,884
They are quite computerized.

123
00:10:10,934 --> 00:10:17,771
China today is perhaps the only country that is deeply integrated into both manufacturing and computer science.

124
00:10:17,821 --> 00:10:20,858
And he feels very confident in both areas.

125
00:10:20,908 --> 00:10:26,315
Well, the good news is that a complete reboot is happening.

126
00:10:26,365 --> 00:10:30,505
The last 50 years have been dedicated to software programming.

127
00:10:30,555 --> 00:10:34,959
The United States is obviously the world leader in this.

128
00:10:35,009 --> 00:10:37,430
But now a new era has dawned.

129
00:10:37,480 --> 00:10:40,208
The computer programs itself.

130
00:10:40,258 --> 00:10:46,081
You just need to show the computer what you want.

131
00:10:46,131 --> 00:10:54,545
Computer programming, all the advantages of the previous generation are now accumulated in artificial intelligence.

132
00:10:54,595 --> 00:10:59,662
This is a complete reboot.

133
00:10:59,712 --> 00:11:06,330
All the digital technology in the world will not replace electromechanical physical technology.

134
00:11:06,380 --> 00:11:09,400
Electrons will never replace atoms.

135
00:11:09,450 --> 00:11:13,181
And that's the advantage you have.

136
00:11:13,231 --> 00:11:21,578
The work you know, the industry expertise you have, the skills that are here, they are here to stay.

137
00:11:21,628 --> 00:11:25,041
But you have to take advantage of this new technology.

138
00:11:25,091 --> 00:11:32,011
This industrial revolution is so grand and powerful that you won't get another chance.

139
00:11:32,061 --> 00:11:33,269
Because you always...

140
00:11:33,319 --> 00:11:34,411
Yes.

141
00:11:34,461 --> 00:11:43,242
Whether we have another chance or not, we must always assume that we don't.

142
00:11:43,292 --> 00:11:44,495
Mm.

143
00:11:44,545 --> 00:11:46,919
This transition is extremely important.

144
00:11:46,969 --> 00:11:58,118
You should combine your deep skills, your deep experience in physical products, in electromechanical products.

145
00:11:58,168 --> 00:11:59,051
Mm.

146
00:11:59,101 --> 00:12:01,758
And combine it with artificial intelligence.

147
00:12:01,808 --> 00:12:11,772
I wouldn't be surprised if most Mittelstand companies are now facing the problem of workforce reductions as people age.

148
00:12:11,822 --> 00:12:15,187
Mm.

149
00:12:15,237 --> 00:12:24,342
The mastery that is recorded in the knowledge and skills of people becomes older with each generation.

150
00:12:24,392 --> 00:12:26,454
Use artificial intelligence.

151
00:12:26,504 --> 00:12:28,226
Let them codify.

152
00:12:28,276 --> 00:12:29,880
Let them program.

153
00:12:29,930 --> 00:12:34,087
Let them train this artificial intelligence and keep it here in Germany.

154
00:12:34,137 --> 00:12:34,498
Yes.

155
00:12:34,528 --> 00:12:37,506
And let him rethink the Chancellor's words.

156
00:12:37,556 --> 00:12:38,043
Thank you.

157
00:12:38,093 --> 00:12:39,494
The Chancellor's words.

158
00:12:39,544 --> 00:12:41,252
Rethinking Germany.

159
00:12:41,302 --> 00:12:42,143
Mm.

160
00:12:42,193 --> 00:12:44,715
This is your chance to do it.

161
00:12:44,765 --> 00:12:56,070
Well, I think it's become clear that the prerequisites for scaling industrial AI in Germany are...

162
00:12:56,120 --> 00:13:02,099
Yes, and they are very promising, and the opportunities associated with them are impressive.

163
00:13:02,149 --> 00:13:12,596
Of course, Chancellor, bridging the gap between innovation and implementation in factories and value chains will be key.

164
00:13:12,646 --> 00:13:18,447
Is collaborating with Nvidia part of the plan for this?

165
00:13:18,497 --> 00:13:19,183
Yes, of course.

166
00:13:19,233 --> 00:13:21,730
And this is already happening.

167
00:13:21,780 --> 00:13:26,558
I would like to say that I am extremely grateful for

168
00:13:26,608 --> 00:13:34,310
what we just heard from this global business leader in AI.

169
00:13:34,360 --> 00:13:42,723
So you give me and, I hope, us great optimism about our business model in Germany.

170
00:13:42,773 --> 00:13:51,240
And you may know that I always insist that we must remain a manufacturing country.

171
00:13:51,290 --> 00:13:54,833
And we should never give up on that.

172
00:13:54,883 --> 00:14:05,681
And now we see a great opportunity in combining our expertise in manufacturing and AI.

173
00:14:05,731 --> 00:14:10,574
So, if we can bring these two aspects and business directions of the future together,

174
00:14:10,624 --> 00:14:17,041
I'm very optimistic that Germany can become one of the most successful economies in the world,

175
00:14:17,091 --> 00:14:23,150
because we already are, we're still the third largest economy in the world.

176
00:14:23,200 --> 00:14:30,396
So thank you for telling us what the opportunity is and comparing us to others.

177
00:14:30,446 --> 00:14:39,290
And I believe, and I share your opinion that Americans made a big mistake by exporting their production.

178
00:14:39,340 --> 00:14:45,875
And now they are faced with a very small, successful, but very limited industrial base.

179
00:14:45,925 --> 00:14:53,584
And this is our advantage— to have broader access and a broader approach to the future of our industry.

180
00:14:53,634 --> 00:15:01,760
And look, I represent a large American company, and we have incredible partnerships here in Germany, and there are good reasons for that.

181
00:15:01,810 --> 00:15:03,311
This is good for us.

182
00:15:03,361 --> 00:15:05,170
This is good for us.

183
00:15:05,220 --> 00:15:09,083
Our partnership with Audi led to our partnership with Mercedes.

184
00:15:09,133 --> 00:15:16,216
Our partnership with Mercedes led to our investment in robotics, which in turn led to a partnership with Agile Robots.

185
00:15:16,266 --> 00:15:18,885
We just made an investment in them.

186
00:15:18,935 --> 00:15:21,570
We invested in Avelios.

187
00:15:21,620 --> 00:15:24,546
I made an investment in Muralla.

188
00:15:24,596 --> 00:15:29,277
We made an investment in Reflex Aerospace.

189
00:15:29,327 --> 00:15:33,603
Last year we invested in Black Forest Labs.

190
00:15:33,653 --> 00:15:39,776
Here in Germany, you have deep scientific and technological knowledge, deep science and mathematics,

191
00:15:39,826 --> 00:15:45,781
which are the basis for manufacturing, deep sciences and artificial intelligence.

192
00:15:45,831 --> 00:15:52,012
So there's so much technical expertise here, so much deep science and deep mathematics.

193
00:15:52,062 --> 00:16:00,047
Another thing you mentioned is that you believe in policies to promote and support AI startups or startups in general.

194
00:16:00,097 --> 00:16:01,988
I think it's fantastic.

195
00:16:02,038 --> 00:16:05,498
One of the things I really love about the United States, of course,

196
00:16:05,548 --> 00:16:09,280
is that we are constantly renewing ourselves, we are constantly reinventing ourselves.

197
00:16:09,330 --> 00:16:11,945
And we do this thanks to all entrepreneurs.

198
00:16:11,995 --> 00:16:17,704
So, one of the things I'm really happy to see is that we've invested a lot in Germany.

199
00:16:17,754 --> 00:16:21,009
We have invested almost a billion dollars over the past year.

200
00:16:21,059 --> 00:16:24,989
This week we are investing in four new companies.

201
00:16:25,039 --> 00:16:27,459
So in many ways we are one of the

202
00:16:27,509 --> 00:16:36,994
largest venture capitalists in Germany because I believe in Germany.

203
00:16:37,044 --> 00:16:45,085
And so all the other venture capitalists better hurry up, or I'll take all the good deals.

204
00:16:45,135 --> 00:16:49,117
And I will get rich.

205
00:16:49,167 --> 00:16:57,308
So, the most important thing I want to emphasize is that Germany's scientific and technological potential is simply unparalleled.

206
00:16:57,358 --> 00:16:59,184
We have a thousand employees here.

207
00:16:59,234 --> 00:17:01,884
We have a great office here in Berlin.

208
00:17:01,934 --> 00:17:07,134
Some of our company's fundamental inventions originated in our Berlin office in Germany.

209
00:17:07,184 --> 00:17:09,954
Therefore, it is worth investing here.

210
00:17:10,004 --> 00:17:11,489
Incredible people.

211
00:17:11,539 --> 00:17:14,142
Can I ask you a question?

212
00:17:14,192 --> 00:17:17,576
Can you give us some advice on regulation?

213
00:17:17,626 --> 00:17:23,456
In Brussels, at the European level, we are having a very heated discussion, compared to the US.

214
00:17:23,506 --> 00:17:28,460
What is the best way to regulate this industry or this new technology?

215
00:17:28,510 --> 00:17:35,691
I think we agree that no regulation is not the right answer.

216
00:17:35,741 --> 00:17:42,912
Too strict regulation is also not the right answer.

217
00:17:42,962 --> 00:17:50,548
But what do you think is the right answer, given the potential harm that could and has happened?

218
00:17:50,598 --> 00:17:54,524
So what is your advice on regulation?

219
00:17:54,574 --> 00:17:57,036
What can we do better than others?

220
00:17:57,086 --> 00:17:58,718
Safety is above all.

221
00:17:58,768 --> 00:17:59,808
Yes.

222
00:17:59,858 --> 00:18:02,759
This industry has no future without safe products.

223
00:18:02,809 --> 00:18:08,632
Just as there is no future in autonomous vehicles if they are not safe.

224
00:18:08,682 --> 00:18:10,701
So it's not a choice.

225
00:18:10,751 --> 00:18:14,271
You don't choose between security and functionality.

226
00:18:14,321 --> 00:18:15,430
They are the only ones.

227
00:18:15,480 --> 00:18:17,898
They should go together.

228
00:18:17,948 --> 00:18:19,735
So, three pillars.

229
00:18:19,785 --> 00:18:25,695
The first is that you can't regulate something that does n't yet exist technologically.

230
00:18:25,745 --> 00:18:30,988
Therefore, the technology industry must Yes.

231
00:18:31,038 --> 00:18:36,408
The technology industry must invent technologies for security.

232
00:18:36,458 --> 00:18:44,161
Just like Ola and I had to invent the technology, and now we have the safest autonomous vehicle in the world.

233
00:18:44,211 --> 00:18:49,652
We had to invent a technology that would achieve the desired functionality,

234
00:18:49,702 --> 00:18:55,051
as well as guarantee the functional and active safety that we were aiming to provide.

235
00:18:55,101 --> 00:18:58,193
So, this requires the invention of technology.

236
00:18:58,243 --> 00:18:59,407
This is the first pillar.

237
00:18:59,457 --> 00:19:02,437
But this is something that Germany is incredibly strong at.

238
00:19:02,487 --> 00:19:06,442
Your science, your mathematics, your technology are of incredible quality.

239
00:19:06,492 --> 00:19:09,131
You have to invent security technology.

240
00:19:09,181 --> 00:19:11,648
Security is not dreams and hopes.

241
00:19:11,698 --> 00:19:14,593
Security is technology and engineering.

242
00:19:14,643 --> 00:19:19,646
Second, companies must follow best practices.

243
00:19:19,696 --> 00:19:27,466
Sometimes young companies are still trying to grow up and may make more mistakes than they should.

244
00:19:27,516 --> 00:19:32,337
We need to make sure that companies are following best practices and methods,

245
00:19:32,387 --> 00:19:39,016
even in the area of ​​safety, as many in the audience probably work in the automotive industry.

246
00:19:39,066 --> 00:19:45,588
You know that security is a deep methodology, deep best practices, and deep engineering.

247
00:19:45,638 --> 00:19:49,310
And this methodology must be followed.

248
00:19:49,360 --> 00:19:54,773
And finally, there are industry and government regulations.

249
00:19:54,823 --> 00:19:59,297
If it turns out that, and remember, AI is two things.

250
00:19:59,347 --> 00:20:06,873
Just like the Internet: regulating the Internet is difficult, but regulating Internet applications is not so difficult.

251
00:20:06,923 --> 00:20:12,161
We should regulate these apps now.

252
00:20:12,211 --> 00:20:19,702
So if there are AI technologies that are being applied to a new type of product that is not covered by current regulators,

253
00:20:19,752 --> 00:20:25,880
they should assess this as soon as possible and make sure their regulation includes AI.

254
00:20:25,930 --> 00:20:35,042
I also heard you say that you want to find a balance between innovation and regulation.

255
00:20:35,092 --> 00:20:41,188
There is nothing to regulate if the industry is not developing.

256
00:20:41,238 --> 00:20:47,463
You want the German AI industry to thrive and also operate safely and responsibly.

257
00:20:47,513 --> 00:20:50,700
So I think this balance is extremely important.

258
00:20:50,750 --> 00:20:57,903
I think the answer is that we know: there are technologies that we can develop and implement to ensure the safety of AI.

259
00:20:57,953 --> 00:21:03,198
We know that there are best practices that we can follow as a company and an industry.

260
00:21:03,248 --> 00:21:07,370
And in addition to this, if necessary, we will provide regulation.

261
00:21:07,420 --> 00:21:07,725
Mhm.

262
00:21:07,755 --> 00:21:11,295
Briefly, Chancellor, what would be your answer to this very question?

263
00:21:11,345 --> 00:21:12,994
Maybe very briefly.

264
00:21:13,044 --> 00:21:13,965
No, thank you.

265
00:21:14,015 --> 00:21:19,024
This was extremely useful as we are discussing this at the European level.

266
00:21:19,074 --> 00:21:25,705
We are talking about this at a national level, and my approach is that we need at least a European level,

267
00:21:25,755 --> 00:21:32,110
and better yet, a global level of regulation and security for this fantastic new technology.

268
00:21:32,160 --> 00:21:43,112
This is my personal opinion, but we are still far from it, as we have different approaches from Americans and Europeans.

269
00:21:43,162 --> 00:21:46,576
But we are working on it,

270
00:21:46,626 --> 00:21:50,754
and most in this room know that I managed to get the AI ​​regulation for

271
00:21:50,804 --> 00:21:56,030
the mechanical engineering industry in Germany out of the general AI regulation in the European Union,

272
00:21:56,080 --> 00:22:00,998
so that it is not part of the general AI restrictions.

273
00:22:01,048 --> 00:22:06,493
So, this was a great achievement that we achieved in Europe. chance.

274
00:22:06,543 --> 00:22:06,916
Yes.

275
00:22:06,946 --> 00:22:07,320
Yes.

276
00:22:07,350 --> 00:22:09,398
Give this industry a chance.

277
00:22:09,448 --> 00:22:10,998
Yes.

278
00:22:11,048 --> 00:22:15,483
And finally, very briefly, Chancellor: if Jensen were to return in a year,

279
00:22:15,533 --> 00:22:23,803
which we very much hope he will, what would you like him to see?

280
00:22:23,853 --> 00:22:27,719
I would like to see him regularly, say at least once a year,

281
00:22:27,769 --> 00:22:39,653
discuss our progress and see him sitting in my office, very pleased with the investment climate in Germany.

282
00:22:39,703 --> 00:22:47,548
Nothing would bring me greater joy than to see all my investments in Germany turn into gold.

283
00:22:47,598 --> 00:22:49,038
Good?

284
00:22:49,088 --> 00:23:00,322
So, I have full, I have full confidence, and I'm very, very happy to hear your personal support for the startup ecosystem.

285
00:23:00,372 --> 00:23:10,201
I am very happy to hear that there are regulations that allow pension funds and venture capital to come to Germany.

286
00:23:10,251 --> 00:23:20,132
And I'm also, I'm also really excited to hear about all the AI ​​startups and startups that have come out in the last couple of years.

287
00:23:20,182 --> 00:23:25,319
Half of the startups in Germany founded in the last two years are artificial intelligence.

288
00:23:25,369 --> 00:23:26,502
Yes, that's great.

289
00:23:26,552 --> 00:23:27,905
This is oriented.

290
00:23:27,955 --> 00:23:28,358
Yes.

291
00:23:28,408 --> 00:23:29,238
This is great.

292
00:23:29,288 --> 00:23:32,429
Maybe next year you'll be asking them for autographs.

293
00:23:32,479 --> 00:23:35,208
You can take the pen when you arrive.

294
00:23:35,258 --> 00:23:44,189
When they achieve breakthroughs, some of them, I understand, used to be your interns, and now they run their own companies.

295
00:23:44,239 --> 00:23:46,146
It was nice to see you here.

296
00:23:46,196 --> 00:23:47,147
Thank you both.

297
00:23:47,197 --> 00:23:49,690
Ladies and gentlemen, please, thank you.

298
00:23:49,740 --> 00:23:54,070
give Chancellor Mertz and Jensen Huang a round of applause.
